# Java Memory Leaks

A **memory leak** happens when a Java application keeps references to objects that are no longer needed.

Java has **Garbage Collection (GC)**, which automatically removes objects that are no longer reachable. However, GC cannot remove an object if something in the application is still holding a reference to it.

So, an object can be:

* No longer needed by the program
* Still reachable through another reference
* Not eligible for garbage collection
* Slowly consuming more heap memory

If this continues for a long time, the application may eventually run out of memory.

---

## How Garbage Collection Normally Works

Consider:

```java
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student student = new Student("Nikitha");

        student = null;
    }
}
```

After:

```java
student = null;
```

there is no reference pointing to the `Student` object.

The object becomes **eligible for garbage collection**.

```text
student
   |
   v
 Student Object

After student = null

student ---> null

Student Object
     X
   Unreachable
```

The JVM can eventually reclaim that memory.

---

# What Causes a Memory Leak?

A memory leak usually occurs when an object is no longer needed but is still reachable through another object.

For example:

```java
List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    students.add(new Student("Student " + i));
}
```

If the list continues to exist and we never remove the objects from it, all those `Student` objects remain reachable.

Even if the program no longer needs them, the garbage collector cannot remove them.

---

# Common Causes of Memory Leaks

## 1. Static Collections

Static variables live for a long time, often for the lifetime of the application.

```java
class StudentManager {

    static List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

    static void addStudent(Student student) {
        students.add(student);
    }
}
```

If students are continuously added:

```java
StudentManager.addStudent(new Student("A"));
StudentManager.addStudent(new Student("B"));
StudentManager.addStudent(new Student("C"));
```

and never removed, the collection keeps references to those objects.

### Better approach

Remove objects when they are no longer required:

```java
students.remove(student);
```

Or clear the collection when appropriate:

```java
students.clear();
```

---

# 2. Unbounded Collections

A collection that keeps growing can consume a large amount of memory.

```java
List<String> logs = new ArrayList<>();

while (true) {
    logs.add("New log entry");
}
```

The list continuously grows.

Eventually, the application may throw:

```text
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
```

### Prevention

Keep collections under control.

```java
if (logs.size() > 1000) {
    logs.clear();
}
```

In real applications, use appropriate limits, expiration policies, or bounded data structures instead of allowing unlimited growth.

---

# 3. Listeners and Callbacks

Listeners can also cause memory leaks when an object registers itself but is never removed.

For example:

```java
eventManager.register(listener);
```

If `eventManager` stays alive for a long time, it may continue holding a reference to `listener`.

Even if the listener is no longer needed:

```text
EventManager
     |
     v
 Listener
     |
     X
No longer needed
```

The listener may still be reachable.

### Prevention

Unregister listeners when they are no longer needed:

```java
eventManager.unregister(listener);
```

---

# 4. Caches Without Limits

Caching is useful because it avoids repeatedly creating or loading the same data.

But an unlimited cache can become a memory problem.

```java
Map<String, Object> cache = new HashMap<>();

cache.put("user1", new Object());
cache.put("user2", new Object());
cache.put("user3", new Object());
```

If entries are continuously added and never removed, the cache can keep growing.

### Better approach

Use a cache with a suitable size or expiration policy.

For example, a cache may remove entries when:

* They have not been used for a certain period
* The cache reaches its maximum size
* The data becomes outdated

---

# 5. ThreadLocal

`ThreadLocal` can sometimes cause memory-related problems when values are stored in long-lived threads and are not removed.

Example:

```java
private static ThreadLocal<String> userData = new ThreadLocal<>();

userData.set("Nikitha");
```

If the value is no longer needed, remove it:

```java
userData.remove();
```

This is especially important when using thread pools because pooled threads can live for a long time.

---

# 6. Not Closing Resources

Some resources are not ordinary Java objects.

Examples include:

* Files
* Database connections
* Network connections
* Streams

For example:

```java
FileInputStream input = new FileInputStream("data.txt");
```

If the stream is never closed, the application may keep system resources occupied.

### Better approach

Use **try-with-resources**:

```java
try (FileInputStream input =
         new FileInputStream("data.txt")) {

    // Read file

} catch (Exception e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

Java automatically closes the resource after the block finishes.

---

# 7. Inner Classes Holding References

A non-static inner class automatically keeps a reference to its outer class.

Example:

```java
class Outer {

    class Inner {

        void display() {
            System.out.println("Hello");
        }
    }
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
Inner Object
     |
     v
Outer Object
```

If the `Inner` object lives much longer than the `Outer` object, it can unintentionally keep the outer object reachable.

In situations where an outer reference is not required, a `static` nested class may be more appropriate.

---

# Memory Leak vs Normal Memory Usage

Not every object that remains in memory is a memory leak.

For example:

```java
List<String> users = new ArrayList<>();
```

If the application genuinely needs the users in the list, keeping them in memory is normal.

It becomes a problem when:

```text
Object is no longer needed
        +
Reference still exists
        +
Object cannot be garbage collected
        =
Potential memory leak
```

---

# Memory Leak vs OutOfMemoryError

These terms are related but not the same.

### Memory Leak

A programming problem where unnecessary objects remain reachable.

### OutOfMemoryError

An error thrown when the JVM cannot allocate the required memory.

A memory leak can eventually cause:

```text
Memory Leak
     ↓
Heap keeps growing
     ↓
Less free memory
     ↓
Allocation fails
     ↓
OutOfMemoryError
```

However, an `OutOfMemoryError` does not automatically mean there is a memory leak. The application may simply require more memory than the configured heap can provide.

